okay last week we had a live caller who  expressed himself maybe in articulately  about the fight for $15 as a minimum  wage and he talked about the possibility  that I guess he said a $15 minimum wage  could be inefficient and it could have  other repercussions that would not be  good in the economy so I thought that it  would be a good opportunity because he  followed up clearly on our reddit  discussion forum I thought it would be a  good opportunity to delve into more  detail about a $15 minimum wage and a  really important starting point when we  talk about the impact of a $15 minimum  wage is to remind you that we are  talking about a $15 minimum wage not a  two hundred dollar minimum wage $200 an  hour minimum wage and that's for a  reason I've explained it before but  there's the slippery slope argument that  some people will make which is hey if a  bigger minimum wage is better and 15 is  better than 12 then why not make it $200  an hour and you can't fall for that  argument when you're talking with people  about the minimum wage a $200 an hour  minimum wage can't be defended in any  serious way it's not an equilibrium wage  by any measure of the economy it's not  an inflation-adjusted wage going back to  you know the creation of the minimum  wage it's not a Productivity adjusted  minimum wage so let's skip the  amateurish slippery slope BS argument of  if a higher minimum wage is good just  raise it even more we are talking  specifically about a $15 minimum wage  there's an argument that is sometimes  made which is you lift the minimum wage  that makes everything get more expensive  and it undoes the impact of the higher  minimum wage and the way it happens is  you raise the minimum wage costs go up  for businesses because they've got to  pay more in payroll  they then account for that by increasing  their prices to avoid going out of  business that disproportionately would  impact small businesses who devote a  bigger share of revenue - payroll than  bigger businesses and are sort of less  able to weather rising costs okay solid  argument right it's a clear argument  increasing the minimum wage causes  inflation and it out does any economic  benefit first of all what is that  argument ignore that argument ignores  that if everybody earned more the  business would likely have more  customers and it depends of course on  the industry but this is one of the main  sort of tenets of supply-side I'm sorry  of demand side stimulus which is people  have more money to spend it businesses  and that's good for businesses the other  issue that's ignored when you say well  you add money to the minimum wage prices  go up by the same amount the problem is  people will make two errors in  calculation one is they'll assume that  if the minimum wage goes up 20% all  businesses have a 20 percent increase to  payroll expenses that's not true though  it only impacts the portion of a  business's employees that earn minimum  wage let me give you an example if the  minimum wage goes from ten to fifteen  dollars an hour that's a fifty percent  increase and then lots of people start  running numbers saying imagine that  payroll costs go up by fifty percent for  all businesses the payroll increase is  only going to be connected to the  portion of employees that earn the  minimum wage if only 20 percent of your  employees earn the minimum wage and it  goes from 12 to 15 dollars that's a 25%  increase only for 20 percent of your  employees which is a fractional increase  to the total payroll expense the other  element to this is that the minimum wage  increase if it's reasonable is going to  be relatively small compared to how much  the prices would have to go up and a  case in point  there was a mcdonald's study I believe  it was McDonald's and I should have  looked it up but I believe it was  McDonald's where it showed that you  could something like double the minimum  wage and that would hit a pretty  significant portion of the employees at  McDonald's but in order to maintain the  same profit they'd only need to raise  the price of a Big Mac by 10 cents why  is that because payroll is only a part  of total costs and minimum wage labor  are only a part of all labor costs  however there's a broader argument  discuss everything I've told you so far  sort of is at the micro level right the  minimum wage change calculation is wrong  the entire analysis other than more  people will have more money to spend is  a micro economic analysis let's think  about this at the macroeconomic level  there's no doubt that some small  businesses would be hurt by higher  minimum wages but consider for a second  the macroeconomic impact the business in  the u.s. is profitable in total doesn't  mean every business is profitable an  increase in the minimum wage will  increase costs at the macroeconomic  level but at the macro level most of  those costs will come out of the huge  business profits that exist it's not  going to put business into the red now  you might say but David here's a  specific business that has a 1 percent  profit margin and that business will go  into the red ok that that's a fair  argument but in total  remember that business is profitable in  the United States profits are  redistributed to the rich through  bonuses for executives when businesses  make big profits salary increases to the  people at the top of the food chain in a  lot of these companies and to  shareholders right when you have profit  in business in publicly traded companies  presumably that profit is redistributed  to people who own shares of stock either  through dividends or through an increase  in the value of company stock that's a  pretty inefficient allocation of funding  those rich people the CEOs that get a  cut of the profit these shareholders who  get dividends they're not gonna spend  most of that extra money they're gonna  put that money away which is not  stimulative to the economy however if  you take some of that profit and you  give it to the employees because of a  higher minimum wage most of that money  is getting spent if you make $28,000 a  year and you get a raise to $29,000 a  year you're likely not putting that  money into the stock market or into a  savings account you're spending that  money  your local community so I think there's  a few issues that are often missed when  people talk about the minimum wage  number one the fact that we ignore the  demand side stimulus of an increase to  the minimum wage number two that the  increased costs can just come out of  profits which will really  disproportionately impact the very rich  you could debate whether that makes  sense but it's hard to argue that that's  not an economically stimulative way to  redistribute money you could say it's  immoral you could say it's their money  let them keep it but the economic  multiplier of demand side stimulus is  significant and that ignores of course  that the payroll costs are only a  fraction of all costs and that minimum  wage payroll is only a fraction of all  payroll so Pat you've been listening to  me rant for a while did I explain this  in a way that it may like your  background is not in economics no but is  am I sort of making sense like do you  have any better understanding of why we  hear a lot of bad arguments I think so  it would also be a good idea for a long  form piece at some point for us and it's  good to point out too that it's not just  those individual workers who are the  beneficiaries of the $15 an hour minimum  wage it's also the economy at large  that's right yeah and you have to it's  very easy to try to tear down some of  these ideas from a micro economic  perspective with very specific examples  from one firm but you've got to remember  business is profitable and we've got to  think about the macro impact here and I  think that this is a good idea actually  for a long-form piece and if I were to  turn this into a long-form piece I would  probably start with a history of the  minimum wage in the United States  because that tells us what would the  current minimum wage be merely if it had  kept up with inflation  what would the minimum wage be if it had  kept up with work or productivity which  has risen even faster in the United  States the cost of living cost of living  is another way that you can adjust the  minimum wage and then we could start  looking into the economic impacts of an  increase to the minimum wage and also I  think it would if we did this as a  long-form piece I would want to tap into  you know you hear from the extreme  libertarians and even I think like  Michelle Bachmann once said there should  be no minimum wage  we have data we have studies that tell  us what would happen if there were no  minimum wage and that would be an  interesting thing to explore as well and  that McDonald's study really sticks out  to me too because if it really is only  10 cents extra per burger I mean who in  their right mind what consumers would  refuse to buy that burger because they  have to pay an extra 10 cents  knowing that every single worker there  is paid $15 an hour well that happened  with Papa John's remember this dude from  Papa John's Pizza who was big against  Obamacare and it ended up coming out  that he would have to add 10 cents to  the price of every single Pizza in order  to give all of his employees health care  so he raised the price by like 15 cents  well I don't know what what he actually  did but when I read that I said wait a  second you're telling me this guy's been  complaining this aggressively about  Obamacare over what would have to be a  10 cent pizza price adjustment to  maintain the exact same profit margins  and I think Forbes looked at that as  well and they're not exactly a left-wing  organization and I think they only found  that it would be 7 cents that case there  was an exaggeration well he was  profiting listen we're rounding up 7 to  10 cents yeah so listen it that most of  these arguments don't make sense and I'm  glad to keep discussing these in detail  and maybe we will look into us look into  this in a long-form format and you can  also participate in the conversation  that already is going on about the $15  minimum wage on our reddit discussion  forum at david pakman.com slash re d di  t the video you just watched was made  possible by support from you please go  to patreon.com slash david pakman show  you can make a pledge of as little as  one dollar per month and every dollar  helps to support the work that we're  doing particularly in light of the  YouTube ad boycott which has been really  bad
 

